[08:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you have any guesses or suspects, let me know in the comments. Moving on, get your bookmark shortcut ready to go because I\'ve got a site for you that I think you\'re going to find tremendously helpful for your next AI film. So, this is Stills Lab, which is well, I mean,'

[08:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of what it sounds like. It\'s a collection of still frames from various films, TV shows, and music videos to be used as a resource. We took a look at something very similar to this called Shot Deck well, I mean, a'

[08:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'number of videos back, but this one actually does have a bit of a secret power as well. So, let\'s take a look at well, Fear and Loathing. So, if we open this up, we can see obviously we have just a'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'number of stills from the film itself. And what\'s handy is if you hop into any one of these, it\'s just filled with a lot of information, very useful information as well, including you know, the color'

[08:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'palette with hex codes, the lenses used, the cameras used, all of which is of course really, really valuable for prompting. Now, there are a couple of different ways that you can go about utilizing a resource like this. You can'

[09:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of course just grab, you know, the shot itself and then bring that into GPT Image 2 or Nano Banana and manipulate from there. Or there\'s a method that I like to utilize. Oh, cool, Dark. Have you guys seen Dark by the way? If you'

